Revise the customized paid pilot brief, We are moving primarily to a zero-call pilot system. Build a zero-call standard package, and a one-call exception package. Identify and fix the gaps to the zero call standard package.
 The customized paid pilot brief is not a draft proof of work. It is a draft pilot plan or proposed statement of work. Proof of work would be evidence that work was completed.
More importantly, the current flow ends with a document when it should end with a decision surface:
Form submitted → PDF downloaded → prospect reads it → uncertainties accumulate → someone requests a call → scope is renegotiated → final terms are drafted → signature → payment
The PDF is informative, but it creates a dead end. It does not let the buyer:
	•	correct assumptions;
	•	accept individual scope elements;
	•	request bounded exceptions;
	•	involve another stakeholder;
	•	approve commercial terms;
	•	sign;
	•	pay;
	•	schedule kickoff.
The highest-leverage change is therefore not another document. It is a personalized Pilot Approval Room that turns the generated proposal into an editable, approvable, signable transaction.
The target funnel
Standard path: zero calls

Pilot inquiry
→ eligibility and configuration form
→ personalized pilot approval room
→ prospect confirms or edits scope
→ standard pilot agreement generated
→ authorized signer signs
→ payment submitted
→ kickoff scheduled

Exception path: one call

Pilot inquiry
→ personalized pilot approval room
→ prospect requests nonstandard terms
→ system classifies and summarizes exceptions
→ all required decision-makers attend one resolution call
→ final terms resolved during the call
→ agreement signed and payment initiated


Build a zero-call standard package and a one-call exception package.
That matches current B2B buying behavior: buyers increasingly prefer self-directed digital evaluation, but still use sellers at critical moments to validate fit, reduce risk, secure internal support, and finalize purchases.
Calls are for resolving consequential exceptions, not collecting information your system could have collected beforehand.
A call should not be used to ask:
	•	What workflow do you want to test?
	•	Who owns it?
	•	Which integrations do you need?
	•	How many participants will be involved?
	•	What does success mean?
	•	Do you require a security review?
	•	Who signs?
	•	Can you approve $5,000?
Those questions belong in the configuration flow.
The call should be reserved for:
	•	custom integration feasibility;
	•	materially altered success criteria;
	•	special security or data terms;
	•	pricing or annual-deployment negotiation;
	•	internal stakeholder disagreement;
	•	legal redlines;
	•	nonstandard liability, SLA, or data-processing requirements.
Gap 1: the brief defers nearly every binding term
The document says:
Before kickoff, Portals and the customer will agree in writing on the annual deployment scope, annual deployment price, pilot-fee credit window, final decision date, included users, included projects, and integrations.
That means the generated brief does not define the transaction. It explicitly postpones it.
The unresolved list includes:
	•	annual deployment scope;
	•	annual price;
	•	credit window;
	•	decision date;
	•	participant count;
	•	project count;
	•	integrations;
	•	final success criteria;
	•	budget approval.
This is why a call remains necessary. Your form collects information, but your output does not convert enough of it into settled commercial terms.
Correction
The generated proposal should contain exact values:

Pilot price: $5,000
Payment: due on signature
Pilot term: September 1–21, 2026
Participants: 5 named production participants
Included workflow: [specific workflow]
Included active project: [project]
Included historical project: [project]
Included integration method: structured import from [system]
Annual deployment option: Studio, $30,000 annually
Pilot credit: $5,000 if annual agreement is signed by September 28, 2026
Final decision meeting or deadline: September 22, 2026

The approval interface (paid pilot form)
If a value is unknown, the prospect should resolve it inside the approval interface before the agreement can be signed.
Do not generate:
To be confirmed during pilot scoping.
Generate:
Required before approval: select a budget status.
Gap 2: the form produces a summary, not a configuration
Your form is extensive, but several questions collect loose text rather than contractual parameters.
Examples:
	•	workflow details
	•	success criteria
	•	security requirements
	•	annual deployment expectations
	•	message
These are useful for discovery but difficult to convert automatically into binding scope.
Use controlled configuration wherever possible
For example:
Historical project
	•	One completed project
	•	No historical project available
	•	More than one required
Integration method
	•	Manual structured upload
	•	Supported cloud-storage import
	•	API-based import
	•	Custom integration required
	•	Not yet known
Customer data classification
	•	Public or published material
	•	Confidential commercial material
	•	Unreleased client material
	•	Personal data
	•	Regulated or specially restricted data
	•	Not sure
Success criterion
	•	Approved asset retrieval
	•	Production-context recovery
	•	Reproduction
	•	Meaningful extension
	•	Knowledge transfer
	•	Variant-lineage control
	•	Continuity preservation
	•	Other
Then collect free text only to qualify the selected parameter.
This allows the system to distinguish:
	•	standard included scope;
	•	permissible standard adjustment;
	•	priced add-on;
	•	nonstandard exception;
	•	disqualifying requirement.

Gap 3: there is no explicit standard-scope boundary
Your landing page describes what the pilot includes, but it does not make the limits operationally precise.
A buyer cannot tell whether the $5,000 includes:
	•	one integration or several;
	•	custom API development;
	•	historical data cleaning;
	•	unlimited asset ingestion;
	•	custom security review;
	•	legal redlines;
	•	dedicated infrastructure;
	•	complex model integration;
	•	additional users;
	•	additional projects;
	•	post-pilot export assistance.
That ambiguity forces a conversation.
Publish a standard package specification. Note: the specification already exists in @PersonalizedPilotPdfDocument and should utilized and expanded, if needed.
For example:
Standard $5,000 pilot includes
	•	One production team
	•	One active workflow
	•	One historical project
	•	Up to five participating users
	•	One standard import or integration path
	•	Up to a defined asset or storage limit
	•	Standard repository configuration
	•	One kickoff
	•	One participant onboarding session
	•	Support during the 21-day period
	•	One final evaluation
	•	Standard pilot agreement
	•	Standard security documentation
Not included without written amendment
	•	Custom software development
	•	Dedicated infrastructure
	•	Customer-managed encryption keys
	•	Custom SLA or recovery commitments
	•	More than one integration path
	•	Complex data migration or cleansing
	•	Regulated data
	•	Bespoke legal or security controls
	•	Additional projects or participants
	•	On-site implementation
A zero-call transaction is only possible when the product being purchased has an objective boundary.
Gap 4: “required integrations” is too broad
The form asks for:
Required integrations or export paths
But the brief repeats:
Integrations: internal storage
That does not establish:
	•	which system;
	•	what data;
	•	read versus write access;
	•	authentication method;
	•	who configures it;
	•	whether an API exists;
	•	whether custom work is required;
	•	whether it must be ready before kickoff.
Replace it with an integration worksheet
For each system, capture:
	•	System name
	•	Integration purpose
	•	Data imported
	•	Data exported
	•	Standard connector, API, or manual import
	•	Customer credentials or administrator required
	•	Portals engineering required
	•	Must-have or optional
	•	Confirmed supported or pending review
Then classify automatically:
Classification
Result
Standard import/export
Included
Existing supported connector
Included
Simple API configuration
Eligible after technical confirmation
Custom development
One-call or separately priced
Unknown internal system
Technical review required
Gap 5: the success criteria are not sufficiently executable
The baseline criteria are directionally sound, but several remain subjective:
	•	“meaningful reproduction or extension”;
	•	“understand its production history”;
	•	“measured reduction”;
	•	“enough to justify the annual price.”
Every success criterion needs five components
	1	Test
	2	Participant
	3	Baseline
	4	Target
	5	Evidence
Example:
Criterion
Definition
Test
Locate the approved asset
Participant
A producer who did not create it
Baseline
Current median retrieval time reported as 20–60 minutes
Target
Under one minute
Evidence
Timed task during final evaluation
Another:
Criterion
Definition
Test
Extend one approved asset
Participant
Named production contributor
Baseline
Current workflow requires reconstructing source context
Target
Contributor retrieves available context and creates one accepted derivative
Evidence
New derivative linked to the approved source lineage
Do not ask “Do you agree with the success criteria?”
Show each criterion and let the buyer select:
	•	Accept
	•	Modify target
	•	Not applicable
	•	Add criterion
Gap 6: annual conversion remains hypothetical
You need to know:
	•	Which plan?
	•	At what price?
	•	For how many users and repositories?
	•	What implementation is included?
	•	When would the annual term begin?
	•	What happens to the pilot workspace?
	•	What is the credit deadline?
	•	Who approves the annual contract?
The annual option should appear in the pilot order form
Example:
Upon successful completion, the proposed deployment is Portals Studio for 20 production members and 15 active repositories at $30,000 annually. The $5,000 pilot fee will be credited if the annual order form is signed by September 28, 2026.
The buyer does not have to bind themselves to the annual purchase before the pilot. But they should acknowledge the price and expected deployment scope.
Use an explicit checkbox:
I acknowledge that the proposed post-pilot deployment is $30,000 annually and that the pilot is intended to evaluate that purchase.
This prevents a successful pilot from reopening price discovery.
Gap 7: budget readiness is currently evasive
This is not an ungated guide download. The prospect is requesting a $5,000 commercial engagement whose price is prominently published.
You do not need to ask for a budget range. Ask whether the stated purchase can be approved.
Replace the question with
If the scope is acceptable, can your organization approve the $5,000 pilot?
	•	Yes, I can approve it
	•	Yes, approval from another person is required
	•	A purchase request or procurement process is required
	•	Funding has not yet been established
	•	No
	•	I am not sure
This is less invasive and more actionable.
Do not include “I don’t want to say.” “I am not sure” is sufficient and reveals a genuine process state.
Then branch:
If another person must approve
	•	Approver name
	•	Role
	•	Email
	•	Must they review before signing?
	•	Would you like to share this pilot plan with them now?
If procurement is required
	•	Purchase order required?
	•	Vendor setup required?
	•	Expected review time?
	•	Required documents?
Gap 8: the buying authority is named but not operationalized
You collect:
	•	production owner;
	•	economic buyer;
	•	technical evaluator.
But the generated process does not require these people to:
	•	receive the plan;
	•	confirm their role;
	•	approve relevant sections;
	•	attend a call;
	•	sign;
	•	complete security review.
A prospect can type arbitrary role descriptions and proceed alone.
Add role confirmation
After the initial respondent completes the form:
	•	Invite the production owner to approve workflow and success criteria.
	•	Invite the technical evaluator to approve integration and security assumptions.
	•	Invite the economic buyer to approve pricing and annual path.
	•	Invite the authorized signer to execute the agreement.
For small companies, one person may hold several roles. That is fine.
The objective is not to manufacture bureaucracy. It is to expose missing consensus before the closing step.
