The security brief is informational. It explicitly says it is not:
	•	an audit;
	•	a warranty;
	•	an SLA;
	•	a signed commitment.
That is appropriate. But a buyer may still require:
	•	pilot agreement or order form;
	•	service terms;
	•	confidentiality terms;
	•	privacy terms;
	•	data-processing addendum;
	•	subprocessor list;
	•	acceptable-use terms;
	•	data return and deletion terms.
A zero-call funnel requires these documents to be available before checkout, not introduced after the prospect clicks “proceed.”
Electronic contracts and signatures generally cannot be denied effect solely because they are electronic under the federal E-SIGN framework, although your exact contract, jurisdiction, retention process, and presentation should be reviewed by qualified counsel.
Gap 11: the final legal and operational consequences are missing
Before the pilot can be signable, address:
	•	Payment due date
	•	Taxes
	•	Refundability
	•	Cancellation
	•	Rescheduling
	•	Pilot extensions
	•	Customer delays
	•	Scope changes
	•	Data limits
	•	Support channel and response expectations
	•	Ownership of customer assets
	•	Ownership of Portals software
	•	Confidentiality
	•	Permitted product telemetry
	•	Data return after pilot
	•	Deletion after pilot
	•	Liability allocation
	•	Warranty disclaimers
	•	No guarantee of exact model reproduction
	•	Prohibited data classes
	•	Publicity and logo usage
	•	Governing law
	•	Order of precedence between documents
These should not all be negotiated in the customized brief. Most belong in standardized pilot terms reviewed by counsel.
The order form should contain commercial variables. The standard terms should contain stable legal provisions.
