This is a lightweight digital deal room, but it should feel like a single coherent transaction rather than a folder of sales collateral.
Standard versus exception routing
Eligible for zero-call close
A prospect can close without a meeting when all of these are true:
	•	One active workflow
	•	One historical project
	•	Five or fewer participants
	•	Standard pilot duration
	•	Standard supported import or integration
	•	No custom engineering
	•	No regulated or prohibited data
	•	Standard security posture accepted
	•	Standard legal terms accepted
	•	Authorized signer identified
	•	$5,000 approval path confirmed
	•	Proposed annual price acknowledged
	•	Payment can be made by supported method
	•	Success criteria fit standard templates
Require one call
Route to one resolution call when any of these appear:
	•	Custom integration
	•	Dedicated infrastructure
	•	SSO or special identity requirement
	•	Data residency
	•	Regulated or highly sensitive data
	•	Custom SLA
	•	Legal redlines
	•	Procurement complexity
	•	More participants or projects
	•	Unusual reproduction requirement
	•	Custom success criteria
	•	Annual pricing disagreement
	•	Missing economic buyer
	•	Conflicting stakeholder requirements
	•	Internal-build comparison requiring technical review
Disqualify or defer
Do not schedule a closing call when:
	•	No active workflow exists
	•	The buyer will not identify a production owner
	•	No credible approval path exists
	•	They expect extensive custom development for $5,000
	•	They refuse to acknowledge annual pricing
	•	They cannot supply usable production context
	•	The use case depends on guaranteed exact reproduction that Portals cannot control

Design the one call correctly
Do not call it a discovery call.
Call it:
Pilot Terms Review
The call should have one purpose:
Resolve all listed exceptions and make a final proceed-or-stop decision.
Before the call
The prospect receives:
	•	complete proposed scope;
	•	exact price;
	•	annual option;
	•	security status;
	•	standard terms;
	•	all detected exceptions;
	•	specific decisions required;
	•	attendee requirements.
Require the relevant people to attend. Do not hold separate calls with the champion, technical evaluator, and economic buyer when one meeting can contain all three.
The agreement should be available for signature during the same interaction. Do not conclude with “I’ll send a revised proposal later” unless a genuinely unresolved legal or technical issue remains.
Specific brief changes
Rename it
Current:
Customized paid production pilot brief
Use:
Proposed Portals Production Pilot Plan
After all terms are accepted:
Portals Production Pilot Order Form and Statement of Work
Remove “why this pilot” from the final contract
It is useful in a sales summary, but phrases such as:
This appears to be a strong pilot candidate
do not belong in the contractual SOW.
Keep it in the approval room as explanatory context.
Replace “should” with defined obligations or targets
Current:
The initial record should preserve…
Use:
Portals will configure the initial record to include the following materials supplied by the customer and technically available from the selected systems: …
Remove unresolved placeholders
Require clarification or visibly mark the document:
Incomplete—not eligible for signature.
Make the value calculation auditable
The 130-hour estimate is useful, but include:
	•	reported frequency range;
	•	reported time-loss range;
	•	number of affected people;
	•	formula;
	•	low and high estimate;
	•	customer confirmation.
Example:

Reported incidents: 2–4 per month
Reported time loss: 2–4 hours per incident
Affected contributors: 1–2
Modeled annual exposure: 48–384 hours
Planning midpoint: 130 hours

Do not present a single number without its sensitivity range.
