Add the standardization message
The $5,000 pilot follows a defined standard scope. You can approve standard terms directly or request an exception. Nonstandard integration, security, legal, or deployment requirements are resolved in one terms-review call.
Add a compatibility check before the long form
Standard pilot eligibility:
	•	One active workflow
	•	One historical project
	•	Up to five participants
	•	Standard import or supported integration
	•	No prohibited or unsupported data
	•	Ability to approve a $5,000 commercial evaluation
What you should not optimize away
Do not remove human involvement where it protects:
	•	informed consent;
	•	accurate technical scope;
	•	security compatibility;
	•	internal consensus;
	•	commercial confidence;
	•	enforceable terms;
	•	customer success.
A zero-call close that produces misunderstanding, purchase regret, or a failed pilot is less efficient than one decisive call. Gartner’s research reflects this tension: digital self-service is strongly preferred, but buyer confidence and deal quality can decline when contextual guidance is absent or inconsistent.
Main landing page
→ Paid pilot landing page
→ Pilot eligibility + scoping form
→ Personalized brief emailed
→ Confirm scope / Request changes / Schedule terms review
→ Final order form
→ Signature
→ Payment
→ Kickoff
Email should remain the buyer’s command center (sequence through Apollo). The actual editing, signing, and payment should happen through secure linked pages rather than attempting to embed the entire transaction inside an email.
implement iterative document refinement?
Yes, but narrowly.
You need self-service refinement because the generated proposal will regularly contain:
	•	incorrect assumptions;
	•	incomplete names;
	•	unresolved integration details;
	•	success criteria requiring adjustment;
	•	security questions;
	•	annual scope requiring confirmation.
But you do not need Google Docs-style collaborative editing.
The prospect should be able to do only four things:
	1	Confirm the proposal as drafted.
	2	Correct previously submitted information.
	3	Request a bounded exception.
	4	Escalate unresolved items to one terms-review meeting.
That is a controlled commercial configuration system, not a document editor.
