The best email-centered transaction
After the form submission, send one transactional email (attio sequence) containing:
Summary
	•	Proposed objective
	•	21-day term
	•	$5,000 fee
	•	Target start
	•	Proposed annual path
	•	Number of unresolved items
Documents
	•	Customized pilot brief
	•	Security and Architecture Brief
	•	Standard pilot terms
	•	Relevant product or integration documentation

Use existing product database (leads database) and existing solutions for other components where possible.

Three primary actions
1. Confirm Scope as Drafted
This confirms that the operational proposal is accurate.
It should not yet imply legal acceptance.
2. Request Changes
This opens the prefilled revision form. The prospect changes only the relevant answers.
After submission:
	•	the Attio Pilot record updates;
	•	the proposal regenerates;
	•	a revised email is sent;
	•	the prior version remains available internally;
	•	the prospect receives the updated proposal.
3. Schedule a Pilot Terms Review
This is reserved for material exceptions.
The scheduling page should display the unresolved items so the meeting has a defined agenda.
Once scope is confirmed, the next email contains:
	•	final order form;
	•	standard terms;
	•	signature link;
	•	payment link;
	•	kickoff scheduling link.
Do not put the complete transaction literally inside the email
Keep the navigation and decision sequence inside email.
Do not attempt to make the email itself handle:
	•	detailed document editing;
	•	security questionnaires;
	•	contractual redlines;
	•	signatures;
	•	payment;
	•	multi-party approvals.
Email clients are poor transactional interfaces. The email should always make the next action obvious, but each consequential action should open a dedicated secure page.
Think of email as the transaction controller:

Email
├── Review proposal
├── Confirm scope
├── Request revision
├── Share with buyer
├── Sign agreement
├── Pay invoice
└── Schedule kickoff

Recommended state model
Create one Pilot record in Attio or your application database. (It may already exist, so review thoroughly).
Inquiry submitted
→ Draft generated
→ Prospect reviewing
→ Revision requested
→ Scope confirmed
→ Exception review required
→ Ready for signature
→ Signed
→ Paid
→ Kickoff scheduled
→ Active pilot

Every email button should transition or update that same record.
Do not create a new lead, submission, or document record every time the prospect makes a correction. The pilot record should remain authoritative.
leverage the existing form?
Yes. Keep it as the initial source of truth.
Your existing form already collects most of the required information. Replacing it would add development work without fixing the real problem.
The gap occurs after submission.
Tally supports prefilled fields and API-based form and submission automation, which can be used to create a separate revision flow tied to the original pilot record.
Confirm or Revise Your Pilot Plan
It should be prefilled from the original submission and show only information relevant to revision:
	•	Objective
	•	Workflow
	•	Production owner
	•	Economic buyer
	•	Technical evaluator
	•	Included projects
	•	Participants
	•	Integration method
	•	Success criteria
	•	Target start
	•	Security requirements
	•	Annual deployment option
	•	Remaining blocker
For each section, offer:
	•	Confirm as drafted
	•	Change this
	•	Request an exception
	•	Ask a question
Should “Scope a Pilot” go immediately to an Approval Room?
No
There is nothing to approve before the prospect provides a workflow, participants, requirements, and commercial context.
An approval room is a post-submission environment.
The correct sequence is:

Scope a Paid Pilot
→ Understand the standardized offer
→ Configure the proposed pilot
→ Review and approve the generated proposal

Sending the prospect directly into an empty approval system would introduce complexity without adding buyer value.
Main landing page → pilot landing page → form?
Yes
That is the correct architecture.
Main landing page CTA
Scope a Paid Pilot
This communicates the commercial destination.
It should link directly to the paid pilot landing page.
Paid pilot landing page
This page answers:
	•	What is the pilot?
	•	Why is it paid?
	•	What is included?
	•	What is excluded?
	•	What does success mean?
	•	What happens in 48 hours?
	•	What must the customer provide?
